{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C161", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1945 10 centavos from Mexico. Value side big 10, CENTAVOS, 1945, national arms reverse. Struck in bronze.", "new": "This is a 1945 Mexican 10 centavos, struck in plain bronze right at the end of World War II. One side shows a big 10 with CENTAVOS and the date 1945; the other carries Mexico's famous national arms — the eagle perched on a cactus, gripping a snake. The coin is small, warm copper-brown, and feels solid in the hand. 1945 was a big year for Mexico: the war had ended, and the country was sitting pretty as one of Latin America's rising industrial powers. President Manuel Ávila Camacho had sent Mexican pilots (the famous Aztec Eagles, Squadron 201) to fight in the Pacific, and the wartime economy had given Mexican factories a real boost. Like most countries during the war, Mexico moved its small change away from expensive silver to cheaper metals — that's why this one is bronze instead of silver like the earlier centavos. This design only lasted a couple of years before being replaced, so it's a short-lived little snapshot of Mexico at a turning point — a confident country heading into its postwar boom, and a coin that actually bought something back when 10 centavos still meant money.", "source": "Numista N# (MX 10 centavos 1945); Wikipedia: Squadron 201, Manuel Ávila Camacho. Note: researcher flagged possible KM#432 vs KM#433 type question — Claude to verify type before merge.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C206", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1976 1 yen from Japan. Young tree / 日本国, 一円 // big 1, Showa regnal-year date (昭和 40 = 1965, 51 = 1976). Struck in aluminum.", "new": "Japan's 1-yen coin is one of the most humble and charming coins in the world — and this one is dated 1976. It's made of pure aluminum, so it weighs almost nothing: exactly one gram, and it's so light it can actually float on water if you set it down gently. The front shows a young tree (a sapling) with the words 日本国 — 'State of Japan' — above it and 一円, 'one yen,' below. The tree was chosen to symbolize the healthy growth of Japan, and in 1976 that was no empty slogan: the country was deep into its postwar economic miracle, churning out cars, cameras, and electronics and reshaping the world economy. The back has a big numeral 1 in a circle, with the date written in kanji as Showa 51 — the 51st year of Emperor Hirohito's Showa reign. The same young-tree design has been used since 1955, making it the oldest unchanged modern Japanese coin design. A coin this light and plain might look like pocket change, but it's really a little emblem of the Japan that rebuilt itself from the ashes and started growing again, just like the sapling on its face.", "source": "Numista JP 1 yen Y#74; Wikipedia: Japanese yen coin, Showa era. 1g aluminum, young tree design since 1955.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C247", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1966 1 won from South Korea. Rose of Sharon (Hibiscus syriacus), 일원, 한국은행 // THE BANK OF KOREA, big 1, 1966. Struck in brass.", "new": "This is South Korea's first 1-won coin, dated 1966 — a small brass coin from a country that was still finding its feet. One side shows the Rose of Sharon, Korea's national flower, with 일원 ('one won') and 한국은행 ('Bank of Korea') around it; the other side has a big numeral 1 with 'THE BANK OF KOREA' and the date. The Rose of Sharon is a perfect choice for this coin: its Korean name, mugunghwa, means 'eternal blossom that never fades,' and Koreans have loved it for centuries as a symbol of endurance through hard times. In 1966, South Korea had plenty of hard times behind it — the country had been devastated by the Korean War just a decade earlier and was still one of the poorest places in Asia. President Park Chung-hee's government was just launching the export-driven industrialization that would later be called the 'Miracle on the Han River.' This tiny brass coin was in people's pockets through the very beginning of that transformation. It was later switched to cheap aluminum as inflation made it worth less and less, but this first brass version is a survivor from the starting line of one of the greatest economic comebacks in history.", "source": "Numista KR 1 won KM#4; Wikipedia: Rose of Sharon (mugunghwa), Miracle on the Han River, Park Chung-hee.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C209", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1987 5 pesos from Mexico. Value side $, big 5, 1987, Mo, scroll ornament, beaded rim, national arms reverse. Struck in brass.", "new": "This little brass 5-peso coin from 1987 is a survivor of one of the wildest inflation storms Mexico ever went through. One side has a big 5 with the $ sign, the date 1987, the Mexico City mint mark, a scroll ornament, and a beaded rim; the other side carries the national arms. It's tiny — just 17 millimeters across — and its design is about as plain as coins get. There's a reason for that: in the 1980s, Mexico's oil boom collapsed, the peso crashed, and prices were rising so fast that the mint couldn't keep up. Coin types came and went like fashion trends as the peso lost value year after year, and the mint clearly didn't expect this design to last long, so they didn't dress it up. Just five years after this coin was struck, Mexico had to chop three zeros off the currency and start over with the 'new peso' — meaning this 5 pesos became just half a centavo overnight. So this coin is less a piece of money and more a fossil: proof of an era when a pocketful of pesos could melt away in months, and a reminder of why Mexico eventually rebuilt its whole currency from scratch.", "source": "Numista MX 5 pesos KM#502; Wikipedia: Mexican peso crisis, 1993 nuevo peso redenomination.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C210", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2000 100 won from South Korea. Value side 2000, big 100, 한국은행, beaded rim, Yi Sun-sin portrait reverse. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "This South Korean 100-won coin from 2000 carries the portrait of one of the greatest naval commanders in history: Admiral Yi Sun-sin. One side shows the date 2000, a big 100, 한국은행 (Bank of Korea), and a beaded rim; the other side shows the admiral's stern, bearded face. Yi lived from 1545 to 1598 and became a legend during Japan's invasion of Korea in the 1590s. Outnumbered again and again, he never lost a single one of his 23 recorded naval battles. His most famous victory was at Myeongnyang in 1597, where his 13 ships smashed a Japanese fleet of over 300 — one of the most lopsided naval victories ever recorded. He also perfected the 'turtle ship,' an armored warship with a spiked shell-like roof, centuries before ironclads. Yi died in battle at Noryang in 1598, shot while his fleet was winning; his last words were supposedly, 'The battle is at its height — do not announce my death.' In a 2019 poll, South Koreans named him the most respected person in their history, ahead of even King Sejong. So this isn't just a 100-won piece — it's a pocket-sized monument to the undefeated admiral every Korean schoolkid knows.", "source": "Numista KR 100 won KM#35.2; Wikipedia: Yi Sun-sin, Battle of Myeongnyang, turtle ship.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C069", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1975 50 cents from Jamaica. Arms, crocodile, Taino supporters, OUT OF MANY, ONE PEOPLE, JAMAICA, FIFTY CENTS, 1975. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "Pick up this coin and the first thing you notice is its shape: ten sides. This is Jamaica's 50-cent piece, and one face carries the island's coat of arms — a shield with a red cross and five pineapples, a royal helmet, and on top a crocodile lounging on a log. On either side stand two Taino Indians, the island's first people: a woman with a basket of fruit, a man with a bow. Below them runs Jamaica's motto, \"Out of Many, One People.\" That motto is a story in itself. Jamaica's arms date back to 1661, and its original Latin motto essentially meant \"the Indians will serve as one\" — a colonial-era nod to the enslaved Taino and Arawak. When Jamaica became independent in 1962, the nation flipped that meaning on its head, adopting the English motto as a celebration of unity among its many peoples. The other side honors Marcus Garvey, the Jamaican-born thinker whose Pan-African movement taught a generation to take pride in their African roots. Declared Jamaica's first National Hero, Garvey's face on the biggest coin in circulation said something about where the young nation was headed. Struck in 1975, this cupronickel coin traveled through pockets at the height of reggae's rise — the music of another Jamaican whose name you already know.", "source": "LastDodo catalog KM#65; Wikipedia: Coat of arms of Jamaica, Marcus Garvey, Jamaican independence 1962.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C202", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2010 10 centavos from Guatemala. The reverse shows Maya stela; 10 CENTAVOS, MONOLITO DE QUIRIGUA, date on arms side. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "This little coin holds the tallest stone monument in the entire Maya world. The reverse shows a carved stone shaft — a Maya stela — and the words MONOLITO DE QUIRIGUA, naming the ancient city in eastern Guatemala where these giants still stand. Quirigua was a Maya city that flourished about a thousand years ago, famous for its forest of carved monoliths. The tallest, Stela E, rises 35 feet from the ground — imagine a three-story building carved from a single stone, raised in the year 771 to honor a king. Every stela told a story: a ruler's name, his victories, the sacred dates, all chiseled in glyphs. These shafts were cut from living rock in the quarry, dragged miles to the plaza, and stood upright as public monuments — part history book, part billboard, part altar. The fact that Guatemala puts one on its 10-centavos coin says a lot about how central the Maya past is to the country's identity. The front carries Guatemala's national arms. The coin is copper-nickel, small enough to lose in a couch cushion, and won't buy much — but it fits twelve centuries of stonecarving in your palm.", "source": "Numista GT 10 centavos KM#277.1; Wikipedia: Quirigua, Maya stelae, Stela E.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "T003", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "An undated Sandy the Pony · Meijer ride token token from USA. FREE PONY RIDE // MEIJER IS FOR KIDS, Sandy the Pony ride token. Struck in brass.", "new": "Some tokens are money; this one is pure happiness. It's a brass token from Meijer, the family-owned grocery chain of the American Midwest, good for one FREE PONY RIDE on Sandy the Pony. Sandy is a legend in Michigan and the surrounding states. She — yes, Sandy is a her — is a little mechanical pony with a bridle, saddle, and stirrups, parked near the entrance of hundreds of Meijer stores. The price of a ride has been exactly one penny since 1962, and it has never gone up. The story goes back to Fred Meijer himself, the company's founder, a man who loved horses. On a trip to a Nebraska supermarket he spotted a mechanical pony ride costing ten cents, and he thought that was too much to ask of parents. So when Meijer opened its first superstore, Thrifty Acres, in Grand Rapids in 1962, Sandy arrived with a one-penny price tag — cheap enough for any kid, Fred reasoned, and a small lesson in paying for things you want. There are about five hundred Sandys across five states, and shoppers have long left spare pennies so the next child can ride free. This token is part of that tradition: a little brass promise of a ride. Generations of kids — and plenty of grown-ups climbing on for a photo — have had their turn on Sandy.", "source": "Meijer corporate history; Wikipedia: Fred Meijer, Thrifty Acres 1962. Sandy the Pony mechanical ride tradition.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C249", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1966 20 cents from Australia. The obverse shows Elizabeth II; the reverse shows Stuart Devlin platypus in water; big 20. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "This coin was born on a historic morning: February 14, 1966, the day Australia threw out its old pounds, shillings, and pence and switched to dollars and cents. In a single day the nation had to learn new money, and this was one of the first coins people ever spent. The back shows a platypus seen from above, swimming in little swirls of water, with a big 20 above its head. The design was drawn by Stuart Devlin, a young artist from Geelong who beat five other competitors in a 1963 contest to design the new decimal coins. Devlin filled his coins with Australian animals — the echidna, the lyrebird, the frilled-neck lizard — and the platypus was his favorite. Look closely at the water swirls and you'll find his tiny initials, SD. It's a perfect pick for Australia: the platypus is one of only two egg-laying mammals on Earth, and it's found nowhere else. The design hasn't changed in sixty years — a rare thing in coinage. The front carries a young Queen Elizabeth II. The first of these coins were struck in London, since Australia's own mint was barely open. Devlin's win changed his life; he went on to become one of the world's most celebrated goldsmiths.", "source": "Numista AU 20 cents KM#66; Wikipedia: Australian decimalisation 1966, Stuart Devlin.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C223", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1967 Dutch 1-gulden coin. The front shows Queen Juliana; the back shows the crowned Dutch coat of arms with a lion. Struck in silver (72% fine).", "new": "Hold this coin and you're holding the end of an era: the very last silver guilder the Netherlands ever made. In 1967 the Dutch mint struck these in silver — 72 percent fine, six and a half grams — and then switched to cheaper nickel, never to look back. A quick trick to tell them apart: the nickel ones are magnetic, the silver ones aren't. The front shows Queen Juliana, who reigned from 1948 to 1980, in a portrait sculpted by L. O. Wenckebach. Look beneath her neck and you'll see his little \"W\" signature. The back carries the crowned Dutch coat of arms — a lion holding a sword — and around the edge runs the old Dutch motto GOD ZIJ MET ONS, \"God be with us.\" Silver coinage had only returned to the Netherlands in 1954, after the Second World War left the country so short of silver that paper notes filled in for coins. This 1967 piece was among the last of that postwar generation before rising silver prices forced the switch. Juliana herself was beloved for her plain style — she famously rode a bicycle through the streets like any other Dutch citizen, earning the nickname the bicycle queen. She spent the war years in exile in Canada, where her daughter was born in an Ottawa hospital room briefly declared Dutch territory so the baby would be a citizen. The guilder carried on until the euro arrived in 2002.", "source": "Numista NL 1 gulden KM#184; Wikipedia: Juliana of the Netherlands. Last silver guilder 1967.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C232", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2003 1000 đồng from Vietnam. Emblem side star, cog, rice wreath, CỘNG HÒA XÃ HỘI CHỦ NGHĨA VIỆT NAM, 2003, value 1000 ĐỒNG reverse. Struck in brass.", "new": "This coin is one of the first to clink in Vietnam in twenty years. By the 1980s, inflation had wiped out Vietnamese coinage entirely — everything was paper, from a few hundred to hundreds of thousands of đồng. Then in December 2003 the State Bank reintroduced coins in five denominations (200 to 5,000 đồng), and this brass 1,000-đồng piece was right in the middle of the set. One side carries the national emblem: a yellow star on red, ringed by rice ears with half a gear wheel below and the full name of the country — CỘNG HÒA XÃ HỘI CHỦ NGHĨA VIỆT NAM, the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. The star's five points stand for the five classes of society (workers, farmers, intellectuals, soldiers, businesspeople) united; the gear means industry, the rice means the farmers and paddies that built the country. Flip it over and you get the denomination, 1000 ĐỒNG. The coin is a small monument to đổi mới, the economic opening that turned a war-torn nation into a manufacturing star — and a reminder of how fast inflation moves in Vietnam, since 1,000 đồng was already small change in 2003. Ironically, shoppers disliked the new coins so much that banks later refused to take them. A crisp example like this one is a fun hold: a brief, hopeful experiment in metal money.", "source": "Numista VN 1000 dong KM#71; State Bank of Vietnam 2003 coin reintroduction reporting.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C203", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 2001 1 rupee from India. Value side रुपया, big 1, RUPEE, 2001, Noida dot, wheat ears flanking, Ashoka Lion Capital reverse. Struck in stainless steel.", "new": "This plain little stainless-steel coin carries one of the oldest symbols in continuous use on any currency on Earth. The Ashoka Lion Capital is a sandstone sculpture carved around 250 BCE under the emperor Ashoka — four lions standing back to back, the way it looks at Sarnath, the spot where the Buddha gave his first sermon. Independent India adopted it as the national emblem in 1950, and beneath it runs the motto सत्यमेव जयते — 'Truth alone triumphs.' The other side shows the denomination flanked by two ears of wheat — a nod to the millions of farmers who feed India, and to the Green Revolution of the 1960s–70s that turned a famine-prone country into a food exporter. Look close under the date and you'll find a tiny dot: the mint mark for Noida, the newest of India's four mints, opened in 1988 outside Delhi. Since 1992 the 1-rupee coin has been struck in stainless steel — cheap, tough, and rust-proof in the monsoon. It also tells you how far prices have climbed: the rupee is now India's smallest circulating coin, the paise having nearly vanished. This 2001 example sat in the pocket of the world's largest democracy right as its economy was beginning its great takeoff.", "source": "Numista IN 1 rupee KM#92.2; Wikipedia: Ashoka Lion Capital, Green Revolution, India Government Mints (Noida).", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C201", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1976 10 kobo from Nigeria. The obverse shows national arms; the reverse shows value 10; two oil palms, kobo, date on arms side. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "Two oil palms stand on the back of this coin — and for a long time, they were the whole story of Nigeria. In the 1800s and early 1900s, Nigeria was the world's biggest exporter of palm oil, the versatile fat that lit lamps and made soap across Europe. But by 1976 the palms were more nostalgia than economy. Nigeria had survived its civil war (1967–70), joined OPEC in 1971, and was swimming in petrodollars as world oil prices soared. The military governments of the mid-70s were building highways and universities with oil money, and the naira — introduced in 1973 to replace the old British-style pounds — was the symbol of a rich new nation. The front of the coin carries Nigeria's coat of arms: a shield flanked by two white horses and topped by an eagle, with the motto 'Unity and Faith' and the legend FEDERAL REPUBLIC OF NIGERIA. Only seven million of these were minted — a small run — and it's dated to a stormy year: 1976, when General Murtala Mohammed was assassinated and the country teetered. Inflation has since eaten the kobo into irrelevance, but the coin remains a little time capsule of the oil-boom years.", "source": "Numista NG 10 kobo KM#10.1; Wikipedia: Nigerian naira, Murtala Mohammed, OPEC 1971.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C258", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A 1953 20 su from Vietnam. The reverse shows three rice stalks tied with ribbon; VIET-NAM, 20 SU, three-dragon obverse with the date. Struck in aluminum.", "new": "This light aluminum coin is a snapshot of a Vietnam that was about to disappear. In 1953 the 'State of Vietnam' — the French-backed government in Saigon headed by Bảo Đại, the last emperor of Vietnam — issued its first coins, struck at the Paris Mint. This 20-su piece was one of them. The three dragons on the front are imperial symbols, borrowed from the old Nguyễn dynasty tradition. The three rice stalks tied with a ribbon on the back honor the farmers' crop that fed the country. But look at the date: 1953. That year France was losing its war against Hồ Chí Minh's forces. Within months, in May 1954, the fortress at Điện Biên Phủ fell, France sued for peace, and the Geneva Accords split Vietnam in two at the 17th parallel. The State of Vietnam — and Bảo Đại, deposed by Ngô Đình Diệm in 1955 — was finished. What makes this coin special is its short life: it's the only coinage of a state that barely existed, money from the last days of French Indochina. Aluminum, simple, and light as a feather — the currency of a country that vanished almost as soon as the coins arrived.", "source": "Numista VN-SOV 20 su KM#1; Wikipedia: State of Vietnam, Bảo Đại, Geneva Accords 1954, Điện Biên Phủ.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
{"ts": "2026-10-15T03:22:36Z", "by": "model:muse-spark", "entity": "specimen", "id": "C231", "op": "set", "field": "story", "old": "A AH 1378 (1958) 1 qirsh from Saudi Arabia. Value side قرش واحد, big ١, Hijri ١٣٧٨ (AH 1378 ≈ 1958 CE), المملكة العربية السعودية on rim. Struck in cupronickel.", "new": "The date on this coin reads ١٣٧٨ — year 1378, but not by our calendar. Saudi Arabia dates its coins by the Islamic Hijri calendar, which counts from the Prophet Muhammad's migration to Medina in 622 CE. Year 1378 equals 1958 by our reckoning. The coin belongs to the reign of King Saud, who took the throne in 1953 after his father Ibn Saud — the desert warrior who founded Saudi Arabia in 1932. One qirsh was a small copper-nickel coin, a twentieth of a silver riyal, in a kingdom still young and poor by today's standards. Saudi Arabia had barely begun to exploit its oil; in 1958 the king's government actually ran out of money from overspending, and Saud was forced to hand day-to-day power to his brother Faisal to fix the finances. True to Islamic tradition there are no portraits — just flowing Arabic calligraphy: قرش واحد ('one qirsh') and المملكة العربية السعودية, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. A coin like this would have bought a cup of tea in a Riyadh souk. Today Saudi Arabia is one of the richest countries on Earth, and this humble little qirsh is a keepsake from the lean years just before the oil boom changed everything.", "source": "Numista SA 1 qirsh KM#20; Wikipedia: Hijri calendar, Saud of Saudi Arabia, Faisal.", "verified": false, "phase": 2, "confidence": "high", "provenance": {"model": "muse-spark", "prompt_version": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS@2026-10-15", "workflow": "story-quality-pass", "assignment": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "run_id": "STORY-QUALITY-PASS-20261015", "inputs": []}}
